
<p class="wp-block-paragraph">For some time now, I&#8217;ve believed that while the Vision Pro is on a slow burn at Apple, its future was certain. The incoming CEO, John Ternus, has <a href="https://9to5mac.com/2026/04/23/john-ternus-explains-what-he-thinks-of-apple-vision-pro/">said</a> several times that he believes this is part of Apple&#8217;s future. </p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">We&#8217;ve heard lots of rumors about them <a href="https://appleinsider.com/articles/26/06/03/short-sighted-john-ternus-behind-apple-vision-project-refocuses">working on a pair of glasses</a> in addition to the immersive product. Finally, the content for the Vision Pro seems to be flowing pretty steadily. I&#8217;m looking forward to the <a href="https://www.apple.com/newsroom/2026/08/apple-mlb-announce-september-friday-night-baseball-schedule/">baseball games we&#8217;re getting later this month in immersive video</a>.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Last week, we got <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2026/08/21/apple-guts-vision-pro-teams/">news of layoffs</a>. And of course that gets you thinking. Right now, Apple&#8217;s dealing with a <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2026/06/25/apple-explains-why-it-raised-prices/">hardware and supply chain crunch</a> where everything is more expensive and harder to get. Vision Pro is the proof-of-concept product that isn&#8217;t mainstream. </p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">I&#8217;ve never been convinced Apple wanted to sell a lot of these, even when they weren&#8217;t dealing with exorbitant prices and supply chain nightmares. Add this to the recent news of layoffs on the Vision Pro team, and I&#8217;m starting to wonder if this isn&#8217;t a <em>slow-burn</em> product, but instead an <em>on-pause</em> product.</p>
